            4. Does Whey Protein Cause Inflammation?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, no. The idea that dairy is inflammatory is common online, but reviews of controlled trials in healthy adults and in people with overweight or metabolic problems have generally found that dairy foods have a neutral or slightly favorable effect on inflammation markers, not a harmful one. The exception is people with a true milk allergy, where dairy triggers an immune reaction, and that is a reason to avoid it entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey specifically has been studied as a possible anti-inflammatory, partly because it contains small amounts of lactoferrin and immunoglobulins and is rich in cysteine, which the body uses to make the antioxidant glutathione. Pooled analyses of whey supplementation trials have found little or no consistent effect on CRP overall, with hints of a reduction in some subgroups, such as people with higher starting levels. That is not strong enough to buy whey as an anti-inflammatory, but it is reassuring evidence that it does not cause harm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When dairy does cause problems</h3>

          <p>
            Lactose intolerance causes bloating, gas and diarrhea, which can feel like inflammation but is a digestive issue, not an immune one. Whey isolate contains very little lactose, which is why it suits most people with lactose intolerance; concentrate contains more. If whey upsets your stomach, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide covers the likely causes, from lactose to sugar alcohols.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skin is the other common complaint. Some studies associate whey supplements with acne in susceptible people, probably through insulin and IGF-1 signaling rather than systemic inflammation. If you have noticed breakouts on whey, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-acne/">protein powder for acne</a> guide covers the evidence and the alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

