                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Add-in</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What the evidence shows</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Worth paying for in a powder?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Turmeric or curcumin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some meta-analyses report modest CRP reductions at supplement doses, often with absorption enhancers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely; powder doses are small or hidden in blends</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Omega-3 (EPA and DHA)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fish-oil doses of a gram or more can modestly lower some markers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No; powders contain little or none</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plant omega-3 (ALA)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only a small fraction converts to EPA and DHA</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A minor bonus in hemp or flax</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher intakes linked to lower CRP in observational studies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, a few grams adds up</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Greens and superfood blends</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Benefits come from eating whole vegetables in normal amounts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No; doses are tiny</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Turmeric and curcumin</h3>

          <p>
            Curcumin, the main active compound in turmeric, has the most research of any anti-inflammatory add-in. Several meta-analyses of supplement trials report modest reductions in CRP, but those trials typically used hundreds of milligrams or more of curcumin, often in formulations designed to improve its notoriously poor absorption. A protein powder that lists turmeric inside a proprietary blend rarely tells you the dose, and a teaspoon of turmeric powder contains only a small amount of curcumin. There are also safety notes: rare cases of liver injury have been linked to turmeric supplements, and curcumin may interact with blood thinners, so talk to your doctor before taking concentrated curcumin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Omega-3 fats</h3>

          <p>
            The omega-3s with the clearest effect on inflammation are EPA and DHA, from oily fish and fish oil. Hemp and flax provide ALA, a plant omega-3 that the body converts to EPA and DHA only in small amounts. Hemp protein is a nice source of ALA and fiber, but it is not a substitute for eating fish twice a week or for a fish-oil supplement your doctor has suggested.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fiber</h3>

          <p>
            Fiber is the underrated one. Diets high in fiber are consistently associated with lower CRP, and most American adults fall well short of recommended intakes. Choosing a powder with a few grams of fiber, or simply blending your shake with oats, berries or ground flax, does more for an anti-inflammatory diet than any extract in a scoop. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-fiber/">protein powder with fiber</a> guide ranks the higher-fiber options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What to Avoid in a Protein Powder If You Care About Inflammation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some of the most useful decisions are about what to leave out. None of the items below is proven to cause inflammation from a single scoop, but each either works against an anti-inflammatory eating pattern or adds uncertainty you do not need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Added sugar</h3>

          <p>
            Mass gainers and some meal shakes deliver 10 to 50 g of sugar per serving. High added-sugar intake is associated with higher inflammation markers and with weight gain, so a powder marketed as anti-inflammatory that carries 10 g of sugar is undercutting itself. Aim for 5 g or less per serving and read the Nutrition Facts panel rather than the front of the tub.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Long additive lists and emulsifiers</h3>

          <p>
            Some emulsifiers, such as carboxymethylcellulose and polysorbate 80, have been linked to gut inflammation in animal studies, and early human research is under way. The evidence is not strong enough to call them harmful in the amounts found in a protein powder, but if you are choosing between two otherwise similar powders, the one with fewer gums and additives is the more conservative choice. Our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder</a> guide applies the same logic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Artificial sweeteners</h3>

          <p>
            Research on sucralose and other non-nutritive sweeteners and inflammation is inconclusive in humans, with some studies pointing to effects on gut bacteria. They are approved as safe for normal intakes. If you would rather avoid them, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guide lists alternatives sweetened with monk fruit or stevia, or not at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavy metals</h3>

          <p>
            Lead and cadmium are harmful at any level, and plant and chocolate protein powders tend to carry more than whey. Consumer Reports' 2025 testing found wide differences between brands. This matters far more for long-term health than any anti-inflammatory claim on a label. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide covers the published results, which is why we leaned on whey and well-documented plant brands here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plant-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Plant vs Whey Protein for an Anti-Inflammatory Diet
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both work. The choice comes down to what else you eat, what your stomach tolerates and how much you care about protein per scoop versus fiber and minerals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins fit naturally with a plant-forward diet. Pumpkin seed and hemp powders are minimally processed and bring magnesium, zinc, iron and fiber, nutrients that are often low in Western diets. Pea-based blends such as Truvani are lower in fiber but mix better and taste more like a conventional shake. The trade-offs are lower protein per serving, slightly lower protein quality for muscle building and a higher average heavy-metal load, which is why brand testing matters more for plant powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey isolate delivers the most protein per calorie, has the best amino acid profile for muscle, and has a reassuring evidence base on inflammation. It is the better pick for older adults trying to preserve muscle, for athletes and for anyone using protein to stay full during weight loss. It is the wrong pick for people with milk allergy, and some people simply prefer to keep dairy low.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many people split the difference: whey after training and a plant or seed protein in a breakfast smoothie with oats and berries. If you want to compare the two in more depth, our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> guide covers digestion, muscle and cost. And if you are building meals around minimally processed foods generally, our new guide to <a href="/best-whole-food-protein-powder/">whole food protein powder</a> looks at the powders closest to their source food.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying Anti-Inflammatory Protein Powder on Amazon: Read Past the Keywords
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search Amazon for anti-inflammatory protein powder and you will see listings stuffed with words like turmeric, adaptogens, superfoods and gut health in the title. Listing titles are written for search ranking, not accuracy, so the checks below matter more here than for an ordinary whey.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the label image, not the title or bullets</h3>

          <p>
            Scroll through the listing images to the actual Supplement Facts or Nutrition Facts panel. Look for the sugar line, the protein per serving and whether turmeric or other headline ingredients appear with a stated dose or only inside a proprietary blend. If the panel lists a blend of 30 superfoods totaling 1 g, each one is present in milligram amounts. Compare the panel with the bullet copy, and if they disagree, trust the panel and check the brand's own site for the current version.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sold by the brand or Amazon</h3>

          <p>
            Check the line under the Add to Cart button. Sold by the brand or by Amazon is the safest option; a third-party seller can be legitimate, but it raises the risk of counterfeit, old or badly stored stock, and with a product you are buying for its ingredient quality that defeats the point. When the tub arrives, confirm the seal is intact and that a lot number and best-by date are printed on it. Plant powders with seeds and fats can go stale, so a date only a few months away is a poor buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flavor and size traps</h3>

          <p>
            Flavor variations under one listing can differ a lot. A chocolate version adds cocoa, which raises heavy-metal levels in many powders, and some flavors carry more sugar than others. The price at the top of the page may also be for a smaller or larger size than the one pictured. Select the exact flavor and size before comparing prices or reading the label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Subscribe and Save and cost per 25 g</h3>

          <p>
            Subscribe and Save typically takes 5 percent off, and up to 15 percent when five or more subscriptions arrive together. It is a good deal once you know you like a powder. To compare products fairly, work out cost per 25 g of protein: divide the price by the total protein in the tub (servings times protein per serving), then multiply by 25. A plant powder with 20 g per serving looks cheaper per tub than a 28 g whey but is often more expensive per 25 g. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> handles the math.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We screened powders sold on Amazon US for sugar per serving, ingredient list length, third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, and the heavy-metal results and brand testing statements discussed in our heavy-metals guide. We excluded Garden of Life Sport and Huel Black Edition because of their 2025 lead results, and we did not rank any product on an anti-inflammatory claim. We did not run lab tests or taste panels; flavor and mixing notes are buyer reports.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a specific condition, our sibling guide to <a href="/best-low-histamine-protein-powder/">low histamine protein powder</a> covers histamine intolerance, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-pcos/">protein powder for PCOS</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/">protein powder for diabetics</a> guides cover blood sugar. People with kidney disease, autoimmune conditions or on blood thinners should check protein and supplement choices with a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a powder for its anti-inflammatory label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The term has no legal definition on supplements, and no protein powder has trial evidence showing it lowers inflammation by itself. Judge the powder on sugar, ingredients and testing, and put your effort into the overall diet, activity and sleep, which have far stronger evidence.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting turmeric in a proprietary blend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Studies that found effects used substantial doses of curcumin, often with absorption enhancers. A pinch of turmeric inside a superfood blend almost certainly falls short. If you want curcumin, discuss a properly dosed supplement with your doctor, especially if you take blood thinners.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting dairy without a reason</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Controlled trials generally show dairy is neutral or slightly favorable for inflammation markers in most people. Dropping whey because of an internet claim can cost you the most protein-efficient option. Avoid it for milk allergy or real intolerance, not by default.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sugar line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A shake with 15 g of sugar works against the eating pattern linked with lower inflammation, whatever its front label says. Read the Nutrition Facts panel and aim for 5 g or less per serving, then add whole fruit yourself if you want sweetness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a powder as a substitute for care</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Persistently raised CRP, joint swelling, bowel symptoms or unexplained fatigue need a medical workup. Supplements do not replace treatment for autoimmune or inflammatory disease, and some, including turmeric and fish oil, interact with medications.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best anti-inflammatory protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No powder is proven to reduce inflammation, so the best choice is one that fits an anti-inflammatory diet: low in sugar, short ingredient list and third-party tested. Truvani Organic Plant Protein is our top plant pick and Thorne Whey Protein Isolate our top whey. Pair either with fruit, oats or flax.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does whey protein cause inflammation?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for most people. Reviews of controlled trials have generally found dairy has a neutral or slightly favorable effect on inflammation markers, and pooled whey trials show little consistent effect on CRP either way. People with milk allergy should avoid whey, and lactose intolerance can cause digestive symptoms that are not true inflammation.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is plant protein more anti-inflammatory than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not on its own. Plant-forward diets are linked to lower inflammation, but that reflects the fiber, vegetables and fats in the whole diet. Plant powders such as pumpkin seed and hemp add minerals and some fiber. Whey gives more protein per calorie. Both fit an anti-inflammatory pattern.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does protein powder with turmeric work?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Probably not in most products. Curcumin trials that showed modest reductions in CRP used much higher doses than protein powders usually contain, often with absorption enhancers. A turmeric listing inside a proprietary blend rarely tells you the amount. Check with a doctor before using concentrated curcumin, especially alongside blood thinners.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein powder reduce joint pain?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no good evidence that protein powder reduces joint pain. Collagen has a few small studies in athletes and osteoarthritis with mixed results. Weight loss in people with overweight is one of the most reliable ways to reduce knee pain, and protein can help with that. Persistent joint pain should be assessed by a doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are artificial sweeteners inflammatory?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The human evidence is inconclusive. Some studies suggest sweeteners like sucralose may affect gut bacteria, but they are approved as safe at normal intakes, and no clear effect on inflammation markers has been established. If you prefer to avoid them, choose an unflavored powder or one sweetened with monk fruit or stevia.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein should I have on an anti-inflammatory diet?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most adults need at least 0.8 g per kg of body weight a day, and more, often 1.2 to 1.6 g per kg, if you are older, active or losing weight. People with kidney disease may need less and should follow their doctor's advice. Our protein calculator gives a personal estimate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

