        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            There is no protein powder that lowers inflammation on its own, and the label term anti-inflammatory means nothing legally. What you can control is choosing a powder that fits the eating pattern linked with lower inflammation: low in sugar, short on additives, tested for contaminants, and blended with fiber-rich whole foods. Truvani is our top plant pick and Thorne our top whey.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Skip turmeric sprinkled into proprietary blends and superfood lists, and do not drop dairy without a reason. If you have an inflammatory or autoimmune condition, or a raised CRP result, work with your doctor or a registered dietitian; diet supports treatment, it does not replace it.
